Q: Javascript Salary Calculator A: <p>The reason nothing happens is because of two main issues:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><strong>Variable usage:</strong> You are trying to multiply the elements themselves (<code>wage_element * hours_element</code>) rather than their numerical values.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Typo:</strong> There is a typo in your final line; it should be <code>getElementById</code> (lowercase 'd'), not <code>getElementByID</code>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the corrected and optimized code:</p>
<pre><code>function calcSalary() {
    // Get the values and convert them to numbers
    var wage = parseFloat(document.getElementById('txt_wage').value);
    var hours = parseFloat(document.getElementById('txt_hours').value);

    // Perform the calculation
    var annualSalary = wage * hours * 52;

    // Display the result with proper formatting
    document.getElementById('results').innerHTML = &quot;Annual Salary: $&quot; + annualSalary.toLocaleString();
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why this works:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>Using <code>parseFloat()</code> ensures that if a user enters a decimal (like $25.50), the math remains accurate.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>toLocaleString()</code> adds commas to the output, making large salary numbers easier to read.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are working on more complex payroll or tax logic, you might find a dedicated <a href="https://usasalarytools.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Salary Tool</a> helpful for verifying your calculation results against real-world US tax data.</p>

Q: C++26 Reflection - How to actually do class metaprogramming? A: <blockquote>
<p>[...] how I can currently inject methods to a class or generate aggregate initializers[...]</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Both fortunately (for the implementors) and unfortunately (for the users), the only code generation facility that got into C++26 is <code>define_aggregate</code>.
We can't yet add new functions as members, for example.
<em>But</em> this doesn't mean there are no new things and that everything was already possible pre-C++26.</p>
<p>My goal with this answer is to provide an example of a plausible code structure and paradigm that works well with Reflection and can be ported to other applications.</p>
<h2>Case study</h2>
<p>Say we have a design that uses a per-object <code>mutex</code> for synchronisation between threads.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so::mt
{

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexed_ref
{
    std::reference_wrapper&lt;std::mutex&gt; sync;
    std::reference_wrapper&lt;T&gt;          data;
};

} // namespace so::mt
</code></pre>
<p>Let's say this code base takes a data-oriented approach, that is:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so
{

struct some_data
{
    std::string  str  = {};
    std::int64_t i64 = {};
};

} // namespace so
</code></pre>
<p>The proper way to have &quot;guards&quot; for each member it is to something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexes_for;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexes_for_t = mutexes_for&lt;T&gt;::type;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexed_ref_for;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexed_ref_for_t = mutexed_ref_for&lt;T&gt;::type;

template&lt;&gt;
struct mutexed_ref_for&lt;some_data&gt;
{
    struct type
    {
        std::mutex str;
        std::mutex i64;
    };
};

template&lt;&gt;
struct mutexed_ref_for&lt;some_data&gt;
{
    struct type
    {
        mt::mutexed_ref&lt;std::string&gt;  str;
        mt::mutexed_ref&lt;std::int64_t&gt; i64;
    };
};
</code></pre>
<p>Before C++26:</p>
<ul>
<li>There is no way to <strong>generate</strong> (without either compiler-specific tricks or macros) <code>mutexes_for</code> or <code>mutexed_ref_for&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
<li><strong>The following can't be enforced by construction</strong>:
<ul>
<li>All <code>mutexed_ref_for::type</code> have all members that they are supposed to have.</li>
<li>All the names are the same.</li>
<li>The order is the same.</li>
<li>The types are correct.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>With C++26's Reflection, <em><strong>we can</strong></em>.
The rest of the answer demonstrates how to do that, and how to write code that <em>benefit</em> from that.</p>
<h2>Prerequisites - Getting data members</h2>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so::detail::meta
{

consteval auto nsdms(std::meta::info type) -&gt; std::vector&lt;std::meta::info&gt; 
{
    return std::meta::nonstatic_data_members_of(type, std::meta::access_context::current());
}

} // namespace so::detail::meta
</code></pre>
<p>This is directly taken from <a href="https://brevzin.github.io/c++/2025/05/02/soa/#we-start-with-storage" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this blog post</a>, written by one of the authors of P2996, <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2069064/barry">@Barry</a>.</p>
<p>Basically a shorthand because it's somewhat long to write.</p>
<p><code>std::meta::access_context</code> indicates what members we get (do we list <code>private</code> members even if they are not usable in this context? etc.). Here, we are working with aggregates - data oriented design. We want and need <em>everything</em>.</p>
<h2>Generating <code>mutexes_for&lt;T&gt;</code></h2>
<p><code>define_aggregate</code> is meant to <em>complete</em> an <em>incomplete</em> type.
The most trivial example is this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;meta&gt;

struct empty;
consteval
{
    define_aggregate(^^empty, {/*nothing*/});
}
</code></pre>
<p>Which makes... an empty aggregate. Notice also that there is no <code>using namespace std</code>.</p>
<p>Using this, we can make traits like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexes_for
{
    struct type;

    consteval
    {
        define_aggregate(^^type,
            /*something smart*/
        );
    }
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexes_for_t = mutexes_for&lt;T&gt;::type;
</code></pre>
<p>What goes into <code>/*something smart*/</code> ?</p>
<p><code>nsdms</code> defined above returns a simple <code>vector</code> of <code>std::meta::info</code>, which each member holds information about a non static data member of the given type.
<strong>It's a range</strong>: we can use <code>std::ranges</code> adaptors on that. Specifically, we can use <code>filter</code> to select only certain members, <code>transform</code> to perform a transformation on the info and create a new <code>std::meta::info</code> with edited information , etc.</p>
<p>In this example, we need only to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Change the type: we want <code>std::mutex</code></li>
<li>Keep the name.</li>
</ul>
<p>The transformation is thus:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>[](std::meta::info member)
{
    return data_member_spec(^^std::mutex, // The type of this member
                            {.name = identifier_of(member)}); // The rest of the information related to this data member, here we just set its name 
}
</code></pre>
<p>We can plug that into our <code>define_aggregate</code> above:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexes_for
{
    struct type;

    consteval
    {
        define_aggregate(^^type,
            detail::meta::nsdms(^^T)
            | std::views::transform([](std::meta::info member){
                return data_member_spec(^^std::mutex,
                                        {.name = identifier_of(member)});
            })
        );
    }
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexes_for_t = mutexes_for&lt;T&gt;::type;
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/ndq514eE5" rel="nofollow noreferrer">And that's it</a>.
We now have a <code>struct</code> that matches 1-1, same name, same count of numbers, but every member is an instance of <code>std::mutex</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>
int main()
{
    auto mutexes = so::mutexes_for_t&lt;so::some_data&gt;{};

    static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.str), std::mutex&gt;); // ok
    static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.i64), std::mutex&gt;); // ok
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Generating <code>mutexed_ref_for&lt;T&gt;</code></h2>
<p>Generating <code>mutexed_ref_for&lt;T&gt;</code> is (only slightly) more complicated because <code>so::mt::mutexed</code> is a <code>template struct</code>.
This is where <code>std::meta::substitute</code> (and <code>std::meta::extract</code>) come in. In this example, only <code>substitute</code> is required.</p>
<p><code>std::meta::substitute</code> is a powerful magic spell that &quot;replaces&quot; <code>template</code> parameters by an arbitrary number of <code>std::meta::info</code> (that must match the number of <code>template</code> parameters), and makes another instance <code>std::meta::info</code> that can be used as a value.
<code>std::meta::extract</code> can then used to &quot;get back the <code>template</code>&quot;, providing we know the type that's in it.</p>
<p>The mental model for these is basically <code>std::any</code> and <code>std::any_cast</code>. You put whatever you want in an instance of <code>std::meta::info</code>, and when you want back not-type-erased value , you <code>std::meta::extract</code>. If the type that's stored isn't what you intended to get, you get an error (with <code>std::any</code>, it's <code>std::bad_any_cast</code>, with Reflection, it's <code>std::meta::exception</code>).</p>
<p>But... For our case study, <code>substitute</code> and <code>extract</code> aside, the logic is the same.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so
{

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexed_ref_for
{
    struct type;

    consteval
    {
        define_aggregate(^^type,
            detail::meta::nsdms(^^T)
            | std::views::transform([](std::meta::info member)
              {
                return /*something smart*/;
              })
        );
    }
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexed_ref_for_t = mutexed_ref_for&lt;T&gt;::type;

} // namespace so
</code></pre>
<p><code>mt::mutexed_ref</code> only has a single template parameter, <code>T</code>, which is the data type.
To get a non-<code>template</code>, we only need to <code>substitute</code> that parameter with a <code>std::meta::info</code> that describes a type:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto as_meta_info = substitute(^^mt::mutexed_ref, {type_of(...)});
</code></pre>
<p>Putting that in the above, what goes into <code>/*something smart*/</code> is simply:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>data_member_spec(
    substitute(^^mt::mutexed_ref, {type_of(member)}), // Template type, replace the template parameters by std::meta::info. If we had more template parameters, they would go between the braces after type_of(member)
    {.name = identifier_of(member)} // Keep the same name
); 
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/96e7GKhrz" rel="nofollow noreferrer">And <em>voilà</em></a>.
We don't even need to <code>extract</code> in this case because we've <em>already</em> got a valid <code>std::meta::info</code> that we can use to declare a member.</p>
<h2>More on <code>substitute</code> and <code>extract</code>: <code>filter</code>ing members</h2>
<p>Let's take another example.
We have these types:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct strange_thing
{
    bool size() const noexcept {return true;}
};

struct another_data
{
    std::string   str     = {};
    std::wstring  wstr    = {};
    std::u8string u8str   = {};
    std::int64_t  i64     = {};
    strange_thing strange = {};
};
</code></pre>
<p>And we want to generate a <code>struct</code> with only members that correspond to members that have a <code>.size()</code> member function, and add a data member with the type returned by <code>size()</code> .
In other words, we want:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct /**/
{
    std::string::size_type   str     = {};
    std::wstring::size_type  wstr    = {};
    std::u8string::size_type u8str   = {};
    // No i64 member
    bool                     strange = {};
};
</code></pre>
<p>How do we do that?</p>
<p>Well, first, we need to have a boolean predicate detecting if we have a <code>.size()</code> member, and a trait to get the type. It's fairly trivial:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so::detail::meta
{

template&lt;typename T&gt;
inline constexpr bool has_size_member = requires(const T m){{m.size()};};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
requires(has_size_member&lt;T&gt;)
using size_type = decltype(std::declval&lt;T&gt;().size());

} // namespace so::detail::meta
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct sizes_for
{
    struct type;

    consteval
    {
        define_aggregate(^^type,
            detail::meta::nsdms(^^T)
            | std::views::filter([](std::meta::info member)
            {
                return ???; // 1
            })
            | std::views::transform([](std::meta::info member)
            {
                return data_member_spec(???, // 2
                                        {.name = identifier_of(member)});
            })
        );
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Now, how do we filter according to a <code>template constexpr bool</code> variable?
The first step is to get something we can manipulate - <code>std::meta::info</code>. The tool for that is <code>substitute</code>, as detailed above.
But...</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::views::filter([](std::meta::info member)
{
    return substitute(^^detail::meta::has_size_member, {type_of(member)});
})
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Won't compile</strong>: after all, it's a <code>std::meta::info</code>, that describes a <code>template constexpr bool</code> - not something that is a <strong>real</strong> <code>bool</code>.
But... We know what's inside. So let's just <code>extract&lt;bool&gt;(...)</code> it. And that's it.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::views::filter([](std::meta::info member)
{
    return extract&lt;bool&gt;(substitute(^^detail::meta::has_size_member, {type_of(member)}));
})
</code></pre>
<p>We went back-and-forth between the <code>template</code> world and the <code>std::meta::info</code>.
I would argue this is very cool.</p>
<p>Back to our example, the last thing to do is to get the corresponding <code>size</code> type. It's <em>exactly</em> the same as we did for <code>mutexed_ref</code>: <code>substitute(^^detail::meta::size_type, {type_of(member)})</code>.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/14qsbxzof" rel="nofollow noreferrer">And there we go</a>.</p>
<h2>Annotations</h2>
<p>Let's push the design a bit further.</p>
<p>This time, let's consider both <code>some_data</code> and <code>another_data</code>.
We'll introduce the following type:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so
{

struct complex_data
{
    some_data    sd = {};
    another_data ad = {};
};

} // namespace so
</code></pre>
<p>With the current design, <code>mutexes_for_t&lt;complex_data&gt;</code> will be:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct mutexes_for&lt;complex_data::type&gt;::type
{
    std::mutex sd = {};
    std::mutex ad = {};
};
</code></pre>
<p>Is this what we want?
Maybe.
But maybe we want this instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct mutexes_for&lt;complex_data::type&gt;::type
{
    mutexes_for_t&lt;some_data&gt;    sd = {}; // {std::mutex str; std::mutex i64;}
    mutexes_for_t&lt;another_data&gt; ad = {}; // {std::mutex str; std::mutex wstr; std::mutex u8str; std::mutex i64; std::mutex strange;}
};
</code></pre>
<p>Or maybe we want:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct mutexes_for&lt;complex_data::type&gt;::type
{
    mutexes_for_t&lt;some_data&gt; sd = {}; // {std::mutex str; std::mutex i64;}
    std::mutex               ad = {};
};
</code></pre>
<p>Or the opposite.</p>
<p>Can we do this without Reflection?
Yes, of course. This will involve a complex mechanism of traits to determine if it should use <code>std::mutex</code> or <code>mutexes_for_t</code>. Good luck.</p>
<p>With reflection, we can add <em><strong>annotations</strong></em>, and just... Choose for each members.
Annotations can be put on (almost) everything (types, members, etc.), and determine if an instance of <code>std::meta::info</code> has a given annotation.
So let's introduce one that says &quot;I want an instance <code>std::mutex</code> for each member of this type, not just for this member variable&quot;:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so
{

namespace mt
{

struct mutex_for_each_t{};

inline constexpr mutex_for_each_t mutex_for_each{};

} // namespace mt

struct complex_data
{
    [[=mt::mutex_for_each]] //&lt;----------- annotation
    some_data sd    = {}; // Will have mutexes for each submembers, i.e. mutexes_for_t&lt;some_data&gt;
    another_data ad = {}; // Will be std::mutex 
};

} // namespace so
</code></pre>
<p>We can then edit <code>mutexes_for</code> to check for that annotation, and generate each member conditionally.
So we have two questions:</p>
<ul>
<li>How do we check if we have a given annotation?</li>
<li>How do we generate a member conditionally based on that?</li>
</ul>
<p>The answer to the second question is straightforward, assuming we've got <code>has_annotation</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexes_for; 

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using mutexes_for_t = mutexes_for&lt;T&gt;::type;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct mutexes_for
{
    struct type;

    consteval
    {
        define_aggregate(^^type,
            detail::meta::nsdms(^^T)
            | std::views::transform([](std::meta::info member)
            {
                if(detail::meta::has_annotation(member, mt::mutex_for_each))
                {
                    return data_member_spec(substitute(^^mutexes_for_t, {type_of(member)}), {.name = identifier_of(member)}  );
                }

                return data_member_spec(^^std::mutex,
                                        {.name = identifier_of(member)});
            })
        );
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>The first question requires to know a bit more about annotations, and specifically, about what they are from the point of view of the language.
The answer is - it can be <em>any</em> value of any (<code>constexpr</code>-compatible) type. That is, annotations are <strong>constants</strong>, with <strong>arbitrary</strong> types. <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2025/p3394r4.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>P3394 - Annotations for Reflection</code></a> got quite a few design changes, but the one that got in was the syntax shown above and just three functions:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>is_annotation</code>, that indicates if an instance of <code>std::meta::info</code> is an annotation (we won't use it here)</li>
<li><code>annotations_of(meta::info type)</code>, that returns all annotations</li>
<li><code>annotations_of_with_type(meta::info item, meta::info type)</code>, a shorthand, that returns a <code>vector&lt;meta::info&gt;</code> of all annotations which type matches the second argument. It's functionally equivalent to <code>annotations_of(item) | std::views::filter([&amp;type](auto&amp; r){ return type_of(r) == type_of(type);}) | std::ranges::to&lt;std::vector&gt;()</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both <code>annotations_of</code> and <code>annotations_of_with_type</code> <strong>return a range</strong>, once again. Which means what we need to do is to check if a range contains an element matching a certain predicate. One way to write it using <code>ranges</code> algorithm would be the following, showcased in <a href="https://youtu.be/ZX_z6wzEOG0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Practical Reflection with C++26</a> by <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2069064/barry">@Barry</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>namespace so::detail::meta
{

template&lt;typename T&gt;
consteval bool has_annotation(std::meta::info type, const T&amp; val) noexcept
{
    return std::ranges::contains(std::meta::annotations_of_with_type(type, ^^T),
                                 std::meta::reflect_constant(val), // Obtain an erased meta::info
                                 std::meta::constant_of); // Projection, only consider the actual value of the constant, not the name for example
}

} // namespace so::detail::meta
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/svKPjoMx7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">And <em>tada</em></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int main()
{
    // Unchanged
    {
        auto mutexes = so::mutexes_for_t&lt;so::some_data&gt;{};

        static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.str), std::mutex&gt;);
        static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.i64), std::mutex&gt;);
    }

    // New
    {
        auto mutexes = so::mutexes_for_t&lt;so::complex_data&gt;{};

        static_assert(so::detail::meta::has_annotation(^^so::complex_data::sd, so::mt::mutex_for_each));

        static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.sd), so::mutexes_for_t&lt;so::some_data&gt;&gt;);
        static_assert(std::same_as&lt;decltype(mutexes.ad), std::mutex&gt;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Using reflection-generated types</h2>
<p>Another great addition to C++26 is <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2024/p1061r10.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>P1061 - Structured Bindings can introduce a Pack</code></a>. As the name suggests, <code>P1061</code> adds the possibility to do <code>auto&amp; [...members] = some_aggregate</code>.
Since we can't (yet) generate member functions, the best call, in my opinion, is to opt for a data oriented approach and use this with addition to (variadic) <code>template</code>s to perform on either the data or an equivalent view.</p>
<p>Let's take <code>mutexes_for</code> of above.
It's easy to define a function that will lock a series of mutexes (simple wrapper around <code>std::scoped_lock</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename... T&gt;
auto scoped_lock(T&amp;... mutexes)
{
    return std::scoped_lock&lt;T...&gt;{mutexes...};
}
</code></pre>
<p>To call it on an instance of <code>mutexes_for_t</code> to lock all <code>mutexes</code>, we can simply first decompose into a pack, then forward that to the variadic template.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
auto lock_all(mutexes_for_t&lt;T&gt;&amp; mutexes)
{
    auto&amp; [...members] = mutexes; // Structure bindings can introduce a pack thanks to
    return scoped_lock(members...);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/K3s6hK9Gj" rel="nofollow noreferrer">That's it</a>: the data-oriented design composes well with such code generation.</p>
<p>However, sometimes, the operation is not as simple as a simple forward.
Let's say one has the (legitimate) envy to write:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
mutexed_ref_for_t&lt;T&gt; mt_ref(T&amp; d, mutexes_for_t&lt;T&gt;&amp; mutexes)
{
    return ???;
} 
</code></pre>
<p>How do we write that?
With packs and pack expansion, it's not <em>impossible</em>, but it's not exactly <em>simple</em> either.
We have two packs that we must expand, which introduces a somewhat weird trick I will not showcase here. And it won't scale well with more than two packs.</p>
<p><em>However</em>, C++26 added <em>yet</em>  another awesome feature: <a href="https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2023/p2662r3.pdf" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>P2662 - Pack Indexing</code></a>.</p>
<p>And this helps <em>quite much</em> with expansion statements.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>NOTE</strong>:
There may be a more idiomatic way to write what's below using expansion statements (<code>template for</code>). I am yet to discover it. This is also due to the limits of that example, because <code>mutexed_ref</code> is not <code>default</code>-constructible.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In our case, an answer to &quot;how do we write that?&quot; could be (purposefully a bit more verbose than required to illustrate the operation performed):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
mutexed_ref_for_t&lt;T&gt; mt_ref(T&amp; d, mutexes_for_t&lt;T&gt;&amp; mutexes) noexcept
{
    auto&amp; [... data_members]       = d;
    auto&amp; [... individual_mutexes] = mutexes;

    const auto impl = [&amp;]&lt;std::size_t... indicies&gt;(std::index_sequence&lt;indicies...&gt;) noexcept -&gt; mutexed_ref_for_t&lt;T&gt;
    {
        const auto make_at_idx = [&amp;]&lt;std::size_t idx&gt;() noexcept
        {
            constexpr auto make_mutexed_ref = []&lt;typename U&gt;(U&amp; member, std::mutex&amp; mutex) noexcept -&gt; mt::mutexed_ref&lt;U&gt;
            {
                return {.sync = mutex, .data = std::ref(member)};
            };

            return make_mutexed_ref(data_members...[idx], individual_mutexes...[idx]); // &lt;------------ Pack indexing
        };

        return mutexed_ref_for_t&lt;T&gt;{make_at_idx.template operator()&lt;indicies&gt;()...}; // &lt;------------ For each member
    };

    static_assert(sizeof...(data_members) == sizeof...(individual_mutexes)); // Passes

    return impl(std::make_index_sequence&lt;sizeof...(data_members)&gt;());
} 
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/GcYG8Gjec" rel="nofollow noreferrer">And this works</a>.</p>
